course: arts
lesson: 41
title: Olympiad: music puzzles ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad block mixes the music lessons into puzzles: counting beats in bars, reading the names of notes on the staff, sorting instruments by how their sound is made, and checking claims people make about music.
explain: Counting puzzles are about beats: in 4/4 time a bar holds four crotchet beats, a minim lasts two beats and a quaver half a beat, so a minim and two crotchets fill a bar exactly, and a rest still counts as part of the bar.
explain: Family puzzles hide a trap: a shiny metal saxophone or flute looks like brass, but the family depends on HOW the sound is made, not on what the instrument is made of.
explain: Picture Ruby groaning, "I've practised this a million times and it still squeaks!" The million is hyperbole, so do not count it; the real clue is the squeak, which usually means blowing too hard or a gap under a finger.
explain: People say it their own way: in the English Midlands "mardy" means sulky, in Ireland "grand" means fine, in New England "wicked" means very, and in New Zealand "chur" means thanks.
fact: In 4/4 time each bar holds four crotchet beats; a minim lasts two beats, a semibreve four beats, and a quaver half a beat.
fact: In 3/4 time each bar holds three crotchet beats, the swaying feel of a waltz; a rest is a counted silence and is part of the bar.
fact: In the treble clef the four spaces from bottom to top are F, A, C and E, and the five lines from bottom to top are E, G, B, D and F.
fact: The family of an instrument depends on how the sound is made: the saxophone is made of brass but is a woodwind because it uses a reed, and the flute is a woodwind because the player blows across an edge.
fact: Brass players buzz their lips into the mouthpiece; the trumpet, trombone, horn and tuba are brass.
fact: In the string family bigger instruments play lower: from highest to lowest they are violin, viola, cello and double bass.
fact: Orchestras usually seat the quieter strings at the front and the louder brass and percussion at the back, and before playing everyone tunes to an A, usually given by the oboe.
fact: On the recorder, B, A and G are played with the left hand alone, using one, two and three fingers.
fact: The kora of West Africa has 21 strings, the Chinese erhu has 2 strings and is bowed, and a modern concert bandura from Ukraine has around 55 to 65 strings and is plucked.
fact: The sitar has sympathetic strings that nobody plucks; they vibrate by themselves when matching notes are played.
fact: Ukraine has won Eurovision three times: in 2004, 2016 and 2022.
fact: Mariah Carey's "All I Want for Christmas Is You" came out in 1994 and first reached number one on the Billboard Hot 100 in December 2019.
exercise: A song in 4/4 has 8 bars. How many crotchet beats is that altogether? | 32 | 12 | 24 | 16
exercise: A bar of 4/4 already holds a minim and a crotchet. What fills it exactly? | One more crotchet | One more minim | Two more crotchets | One semibreve
exercise: In 3/4 time, how many quavers fill one whole bar? | 6 | 3 | 4 | 8
exercise: Read the four spaces of the treble clef from the bottom up. Which word do they spell? | FACE | CAFE | FADE | BEAD
exercise: On the treble staff, a tune goes: the middle line, then the second space up, then the second line up. Which word do the note names spell? | BAG | BED | GAB | DAB
exercise: Ivy plays B, then A, then G on her recorder. How many left-hand fingers go down, counting all three notes together? | 6 | 3 | 9 | 5
exercise: Odd one out: trumpet, tuba, saxophone, trombone. Which one, and why? | The saxophone, because it uses a reed | The tuba, because it is the biggest | The trumpet, it plays highest | The trombone, because it has a slide
exercise: Put these from highest sound to lowest: cello, violin, double bass, viola. | Violin, viola, cello, double bass | Double bass, cello, viola, violin | Viola, violin, double bass, cello | Violin, cello, viola, double bass
exercise: A new player is told to sit right at the back of the orchestra. Which instrument is she most likely to play? | The trombone | The first violin | The viola | The cello
exercise: The oboe plays the tuning note. On the treble staff, where is that note written if it sits in a space? | The second space up | The bottom space of the staff | The top space of the staff | The third space from the bottom
exercise: A kora and two erhus are on the table. How many strings is that in total? | 25 | 23 | 42 | 21
exercise: Which strings of a sitar make sound even though nobody touches them? | The sympathetic strings, which vibrate along with matching notes | The bass strings, which the player taps with a little stick | The top strings, which the wind blows across like a flute | None of them, because every string must be plucked to sound
exercise: How many years passed between Ukraine's first and third Eurovision wins? | 18 | 12 | 6 | 22
exercise: Nan says the Mariah Carey Christmas song "took forever" to reach number one. How long did it really take? | 25 years | 19 years | 15 years | Forever, it never did
exercise: Which pair belongs to the same family, even though one is wood and one is metal? | A clarinet and a saxophone | A trumpet and a saxophone | A violin and a piano | A tuba and a cello
exercise: Which of these bars of 4/4 is too LONG? | Minim, minim, crotchet | Minim, crotchet, crotchet | Crotchet, crotchet rest, minim | Four crotchets in a row
test: "bruv, our song's in 3/4, 16 bars, how many beats is that innit" | 48 | 64 | 19 | 32
test: "the song's got 6 bars of 4/4, no wait, 7 bars. how many minims fill the lot" | 14 | 12 | 28 | 7
test: Jade is mardy ("mardy" means sulky) because her flute is metal and her teacher calls it a woodwind. Who is right? | The teacher: it is how the sound is made | Jade, because metal instruments always belong to the brass family | Jade, because a woodwind has to be made of real wood | Neither, because the flute is really a kind of percussion
test: Three of these are usually played with a bow. Which odd one is plucked? | The kora | The Chinese erhu | The violin | The cello
test: Liam reckons, "Bigger kid, lower notes, so the tall boy gets the tuba." What is the real rule? | The instrument's size sets how low it plays, not the player's height | Tall players always make the lowest sounds on any instrument | Only tall people are allowed to play the tuba in an orchestra | The tuba plays highest, so the smallest kid should get it
test: After the gig the drummer says, "Ears are a bit buzzy, no biggie." What does that really mean? | Too loud for his ears; earplugs would help | Nothing at all, buzzing ears are a sign of a really good gig | His drums are broken and need new skins before the next gig | He is joking, ears cannot be hurt by music however loud it is
test: Put the steps of making a song recording in order. | A demo, then recording, then mixing, then mastering | Mastering, then a demo, then recording, then mixing | A demo, then mixing, then recording, then mastering | Recording, then mastering, then mixing, then a demo
test: A bar of 4/4 holds a crotchet, a crotchet rest and a minim. Sam says the rest doesn't count, so the bar is short. Is he right? | No: 1 + 1 + 2 makes 4 beats, the rest counts | Yes: the bar holds only 3 beats | Yes: a rest lasts no time at all | No: the bar holds 5 beats, so it is too long
test: A kid shouts, "The bandura's got like a thousand strings, no cap!" ("no cap" means honestly). About how many does a concert bandura really have? | About 55 to 65 | Exactly 21 | Only 2 | About a thousand
test: The mic squeals louder and louder, and the soundman from Cork says, "Ah, it's grand." What is really happening? | The mic picks up the speaker in a loop, so turn it away | Everything is fine, the squeal is part of the song | The singer's voice is too high for the microphone | The batteries in the mic are about to run out
test: Ukraine won Eurovision in 2004, 2016 and 2022: gaps of 12 years, then 6. Max says, "Halves every time, so the next win is wicked sure in 2025." ("wicked" means very). What is wrong? | Contest wins are not a number pattern that must go on | Nothing, the gaps always halve, so 2025 is certain | His sum is wrong: half of 6 makes the next win 2028 | The pattern is right, but Eurovision stopped in 2022
test: Ruby: "Practised a million times and it still squeaks!" What should she check first? | Soft, steady breath and flat finger pads over the holes | Practising a million more times, changing nothing | Blowing much harder so the squeak gets drowned out | Pressing the holes with her hard fingertips instead
exam: Determine the number of crotchet beats contained in twelve bars of three-four time. | Thirty-six | Fifteen | Forty-eight | Twelve
exam: Identify the principle by which the family of a musical instrument is determined. | The manner in which its sound is produced | The material of which it is constructed | The size of the person who plays it | The country in which it was invented
exam: Select the word spelled by the notes of the four spaces of the treble clef, read from the lowest upward. | FACE | CAFE | EGBDF | DEAF
exam: Ascertain which of the following arrangements of the string family proceeds correctly from the highest sound to the lowest. | Violin, viola, cello, double bass | Double bass, cello, viola, violin | Viola, violin, cello, double bass | Cello, violin, viola, double bass
